WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.025
Check yourself with two questions.

2
00:00:02.025 --> 00:00:07.113
First: which two Attack Surface Reduction
rules go from audit straight to block?

3
00:00:07.113 --> 00:00:11.630
The answer is a: the rule that blocks
credential stealing from the local security

4
00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:15.069
authority, and the rule that
blocks Office applications from

5
00:00:15.069 --> 00:00:18.912
injecting code into other processes,
because neither supports warn.

6
00:00:18.912 --> 00:00:23.213
Second: a Windows Server shows
a rule as compliant in Intune.

7
00:00:23.213 --> 00:00:24.612
Is that enough evidence?

8
00:00:24.612 --> 00:00:26.151
The answer is b, no.

9
00:00:26.151 --> 00:00:30.933
Microsoft lists a known issue where the
server shows compliant without the rule

10
00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:33.574
being enforced, so check
on the server itself,

11
00:00:33.574 --> 00:00:35.787
with Get M P Preference and the events.

12
00:00:35.787 --> 00:00:39.938
Setting the rule in Group Policy
too would only add the other danger.

13
00:00:39.938 --> 00:00:44.900
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.
